## Building the target workbook

Once a query result CSV comes back, build country-level target tables in
`.xlsx` following the `xlsx` skill's conventions (openpyxl, real formulas not
hardcoded values, Arial font). Sort by improvement potential (actual DT60 minus
target DT60) descending so the biggest opportunities surface first. If you're
in a sandboxed environment where LibreOffice recalculation is too slow to
finish before a tool-call timeout, don't fight it repeatedly -- write the
formula's already-known result directly into the cell's cached `<v>` value
alongside the `<f>` formula (standard OOXML: real Excel files always carry
both), verified by reloading with `data_only=True` and checking the cached
value matches a from-scratch recomputation. This gives you a file that opens
correctly in Excel/LibreOffice/Numbers with live formulas, without needing a
working LibreOffice recalc pass in this session.
